          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "പുറപ്പെടുക (puṟappeṭuka) — \"to set out\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A compound with its seam showing. പുറം (puṟaṁ) is 'the outside, the back'; Tamil has புறம் with the same sense. The second piece is the ordinary verb of falling or happening. Together, roughly, 'to come to be outside' — which is what setting out is."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malayalam builds a great many verbs this way, a noun and a light verb sitting next to each other, and this book has already shown one: the work-verb made of 'work' plus 'do'."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada says ಹೊರಡು (horaḍu), built on its own outside-word, and that word begins with the h where ours begins with a p. The shift turns up again."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four. Now, the day after tomorrow, a journey, setting out."
            }
          ]
        },
