      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did puṟappeṭuka mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: വിട",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "വിട (viṭa) — \"leave, a farewell\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A short word off a plain verb: വിടുക (viṭuka) is 'to let go, to release'. വിട (viṭa) is the letting-go itself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And it is used exactly as the journey-word was: വിട (viṭa) പറയുക (paṟayuka), 'to say farewell'. Malayalam has two ways to announce a departure, one naming the going and one naming the release, and both are built by hanging the speaking-verb off a noun."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada's word here, ಬೀಳ್ಕೊಡುಗೆ (bīḷkoḍuge), is longer and made of its own native parts — a giving of leave. Three languages could hardly agree less on how to say goodbye, and each of them is transparent once opened."
            }
